# Hand-written deepening modules, batch B (own wording).
# Each: (title, bloom, hours, intro, [points], example, activity, standard)
D2 = {
"pw-06-ai-data-science": [
 ("Test a model honestly on unseen data","Apply",10,"A model that scores well on the data it learned from may fail on new data. An honest test keeps some records hidden until the end.",
  ["Split the records before you start: for example 70% to train, 15% to tune and 15% to test. Never look at the test part while building.","Pick a measure that fits the question: accuracy for balanced groups, but precision (how many flagged cases were truly positive) and recall (how many true positives were found) when one outcome is rare.","Compare the model with a simple baseline, such as always predicting the most common answer. A model that cannot beat the baseline is not useful.","Write down the split, the measure and the result before you change anything, so you cannot fool yourself later."],
  "A set of 2,000 customer records is split 1,400 / 300 / 300. The model gets 288 of the 300 test cases right: 288 / 300 = 96.0%. But 95% of the 300 test customers (285 of them) never cancel, so the baseline of always saying 'stays' already scores 95.0%. The gain is only 1.0 percentage point, so the model adds little.",
  "Take a sample table of at least 500 rows from your mentor, split it, run a simple model and a baseline, and report both scores.","Write down the split sizes, show the baseline score next to the model score, and leave the test part untouched until the final run."),
 ("Bias, privacy and rules for using data","Evaluate",9,"Data about people can harm them if it is unfair, leaked or used for a purpose they never agreed to. Checking this is part of the job, not an extra.",
  ["Check error rates for each group, such as age band or nationality group. If one group is wrong far more often, the model is unfair even when the overall score is high.","Collect only the fields you need. Remove names and ID numbers before analysis where you can, and store the key to re-link them somewhere separate.","Know the purpose: data collected for one reason should not be reused for another without permission and a lawful basis.","Keep a short record of where the data came from, who may see it and when it will be deleted."],
  "A loan-screening model is wrong 6% of the time for group A (30 errors in 500 cases) but 15% for group B (75 errors in 500 cases). The gap is 9 percentage points, so the model must be reviewed before use, even though the overall error is 10.5% (105 in 1,000 cases).",
  "Calculate error rate per group from a sample results table and write a half-page note: fair enough to use, or not, and why.","Show the error rate for every group, justify the data fields one by one, and give a clear recommendation with reasons."),
 ("Explain results and keep watching the model","Evaluate",9,"A good result is only useful if the decision-maker understands it and if it keeps working after launch. Data changes, and models slowly go stale.",
  ["Lead with the answer in one sentence, then give the number, then the limits. Avoid jargon; say 'wrong about 1 time in 10' instead of a technical score.","Show uncertainty: give a range, not a single figure, and say how many records it rests on.","Monitor after launch: track the live error rate each week against the test result and set a trigger, for example retrain if it drops more than 5 percentage points.","Keep a log of model versions, data dates and who approved each change."],
  "At launch the model was right 92% of the time. In week 6, a check of 400 live cases finds 352 right: 352 / 400 = 88.0%. The drop is 4 percentage points, close to the 5-point trigger, so the team schedules a review of the new data.",
  "Write a one-page summary for a non-technical manager using the results from module 1, with one chart and one monitoring rule.","Answer in the first sentence; range or limit stated; a numeric monitoring trigger set."),
],
"pw-07-cloud-cybersecurity": [
 ("Size, cost and protect a cloud set-up","Apply",10,"Cloud services are rented by the hour, so a plan needs sums for capacity, cost and backup. Security starts with knowing exactly what you are running.",
  ["List every asset first: servers, storage, databases, user accounts and who owns each. You cannot protect what you have not listed.","Estimate monthly cost as price per hour x hours running x number of machines, then add storage and data transfer.","Back up by the 3-2-1 idea: 3 copies, on 2 kinds of storage, 1 held off-site. Set how much data you can afford to lose (in hours) and how fast you must be back (in hours).","Test a restore, not just the backup. A backup never restored is only a hope."],
  "A small office runs 2 servers for 730 hours a month at AED 0.40 per hour each: 2 x 730 x 0.40 = AED 584 per month. Storage of 500 GB at AED 0.09 per GB per month = AED 45 per month. Total about AED 629 per month. Switching one test server off for 16 hours each night saves 16 x 30 x 0.40 = AED 192 per month.",
  "Build a cost sheet for a sample set-up of 3 machines and 200 GB storage, and write the restore-test steps for it.","List every asset with an owner, compute monthly cost with units, and describe and date one restore test."),
 ("Access control, attacks and permission to test","Evaluate",9,"Most break-ins use stolen or weak passwords, not clever tricks. Good control and clear legal limits protect both the client and you.",
  ["Give each person least privilege: only the access their job needs, removed on the day they leave.","Turn on multi-factor sign-in for every account that can reach data or change settings; start with administrators.","Recognise common attacks: phishing messages, ransom software, weak or reused passwords, and unpatched systems.","Only test or scan systems where you hold written permission that states the scope and dates. Testing without permission can be a crime."],
  "A review of 40 accounts finds 6 without multi-factor sign-in (15.0%) and 4 belonging to staff who have left (10.0%). Fix order: disable the 4 leaver accounts today, then enable multi-factor sign-in on the 6, starting with the 2 administrators.",
  "Review a sample list of 30 accounts supplied by your mentor and produce a ranked fix list with owners and dates.","Fix leaver accounts and administrators first, date the fixes and touch no live system without written permission."),
 ("Detect, respond and measure an incident","Analyse",9,"When something goes wrong, speed matters. Teams measure how long it takes to notice and to fix, then try to shorten both.",
  ["Collect logs from sign-ins, firewalls and servers in one place, with clocks set to the same time.","Follow the response steps in order: detect, contain, remove the cause, recover, then review what happened.","Record times: time to detect and time to fix, both in minutes, for every incident.","Hold a short review after each incident and write down one change that would have stopped or shortened it."],
  "Incident A: started 09:00, noticed 09:45, fixed 12:15. Time to detect = 45 minutes; time to fix = 150 minutes after detection. Incident B: detected in 20 minutes and fixed in 90 minutes. Averages: detect (45 + 20) / 2 = 32.5 minutes; fix (150 + 90) / 2 = 120 minutes.",
  "Calculate average time to detect and fix from a sample incident log of 5 events and suggest one change for each.","Calculate times in minutes, show steps in the right order and give each review one concrete change."),
],
"pw-08-venture-private-equity": [
 ("Read a deal: valuation and ownership maths","Apply",10,"Every investment deal comes down to a few numbers: what the firm is worth before money arrives, how much is put in, and what share that buys.",
  ["Pre-money value is the worth of the firm before new money. Post-money value = pre-money value + the new investment.","The new investor's share = investment / post-money value. Existing owners are diluted by the same proportion.","A term sheet lists the main terms: amount, valuation, share type, board seats and rights if things go badly. It is a summary, not the final contract.","Investments can lose all their value. Always show the downside case next to any upside case, and never state returns as certain."],
  "A start-up has a pre-money value of AED 8,000,000. An investor puts in AED 2,000,000. Post-money = 8,000,000 + 2,000,000 = AED 10,000,000. Investor share = 2,000,000 / 10,000,000 = 20.0%. A founder who held 100% now holds 80.0%.",
  "Calculate ownership before and after a round for three sample deals and show the downside case for each.","Show pre- and post-money, check that shares add to 100%, and write a downside case for every deal."),
 ("Due diligence, conflicts and honest reporting","Evaluate",9,"Checking the facts behind a deal protects the money of other people. So does declaring any personal interest early.",
  ["Due diligence covers four areas: financial records, legal ownership and contracts, customers and market, and the people running the firm.","Ask for evidence, not claims: bank statements, signed contracts, tax filings and licences.","Declare conflicts of interest in writing, such as a relative or an existing stake in a rival, before a deal is discussed.","Report performance fairly: show losses as well as gains, and never promise or imply future returns. Only licensed professionals give investment advice."],
  "A firm claims sales of AED 5,000,000 a year. Bank statements show deposits of AED 3,900,000 over the same 12 months. Gap = AED 1,100,000, which is 22.0% of the claim. The deal pauses until the firm explains the gap in writing.",
  "Prepare a due diligence checklist of at least 12 evidence items for a sample firm and mark which are missing.","Name the evidence document for each item, measure the gaps, and complete the conflict declaration form."),
 ("Screen deals and review the portfolio","Analyse",9,"A team sees many more deals than it can fund. Simple counts show where the process is working and where it leaks.",
  ["Set screening rules before looking at deals: sector, stage, size of investment and minimum evidence.","Track the funnel: deals received, passed first screen, reached diligence, funded.","In a portfolio review, mark each holding as on plan, watch or at risk, with a reason and a next step.","Remember a few investments often lose money; judge the whole portfolio and the quality of decisions, not one result."],
  "In a year: 240 deals received, 60 passed screening, 15 reached diligence, 5 funded. Screening pass rate = 60 / 240 = 25.0%. Deals screened per deal funded = 240 / 5 = 48. Diligence to funded = 5 / 15 = 33.3%.",
  "Build a funnel table from sample figures and write a one-line review note for each of 6 sample holdings.","Calculate percentages correct to one decimal place, give every holding a status, reason and next step, and promise no return."),
],
"pw-09-esg-sustainability-governance": [
 ("Measure emissions: baseline and calculation","Apply",10,"You cannot cut what you have not counted. A baseline gives the starting number against which later progress is compared.",
  ["Scope 1 is direct emissions, such as fuel burned in company vehicles. Scope 2 is emissions from bought electricity.","Method: activity amount x emission factor = emissions. Use a published factor and write down its source and year. CO2e means carbon dioxide equivalent, one unit that covers all greenhouse gases; kWh means kilowatt-hour, the unit of electricity use.","Choose a baseline year with complete records and keep the method the same each year so results can be compared.","Record the data source for every figure: invoice, meter reading or fuel card. Estimates are marked as estimates."],
  "An office uses 120,000 kWh of electricity in a year. With a factor of 0.40 kg of CO2e per kWh: 120,000 x 0.40 = 48,000 kg = 48.0 tonnes CO2e. Fleet fuel is 9,000 litres at 2.3 kg per litre (a petrol-type factor; use the factor for your own fuel, diesel is higher) = 20,700 kg = 20.7 tonnes. Total = 68.7 tonnes CO2e.",
  "Compute a baseline for a sample site from a table of utility and fuel data and list the source of each number.","State the factor, source and year; give units on every line; mark estimates clearly."),
 ("Materiality, greenwashing and assurance","Evaluate",9,"Reports lose trust when they exaggerate. Choosing the right issues and backing every claim with evidence keeps a report honest.",
  ["Materiality means focusing on issues that matter most to the firm and the people it affects. Ask staff, customers, lenders and neighbours, then rank the issues.","Greenwashing is making a claim the evidence does not support, such as calling a product green without data. Test each claim: what, how much, compared with what, and where is the proof?","Assurance is an independent check of the report. Limited assurance gives a lower level of comfort than reasonable assurance.","Report bad news too. Leaving out targets that were missed is itself misleading."],
  "A claim says 'we cut emissions by half'. The records show 80.0 tonnes in the baseline year and 66.0 tonnes now. Cut = 14.0 tonnes = 17.5%, not 50%. The wording must change to 'a 17.5% reduction against the baseline year'.",
  "Check five sample claims from a draft report against the data table and rewrite each one so it matches the evidence.","Support every claim with a figure, a comparison and a source; remove or reword unsupported claims."),
 ("Targets, governance and progress review","Analyse",9,"A target only works if someone owns it, a date is set and progress is checked on a schedule.",
  ["Set targets as a number, a unit, a baseline and a date, for example a 20% cut by year 5.","Name an owner for each target and a board or committee that reviews progress at least twice a year.","Compare actual with the straight-line path to the target, and explain any gap.","Update the plan when evidence shows a measure is not working; record what changed and why."],
  "Baseline 68.7 tonnes CO2e. Target: 20% cut in 4 years = 13.74 tonnes, so 54.96 tonnes. The straight-line path is 3.435 tonnes per year, so after year 2 the path value is 61.83 tonnes. Actual is 64.0 tonnes, which is 2.17 tonnes behind the path, so the committee asks for extra measures.",
  "Draw a target path for a sample site, plot two years of actual figures and write a short note on the gap.","State the target with baseline and date, calculate the gap in the same unit, and name the owner by role, not by person."),
],
"pw-10-ppp-governance": [
 ("Test value for money and compare options","Apply",10,"Before a public body signs a long partnership, it must show that the partnership is better than doing the project another way.",
  ["List the options: public delivery, a partnership, or doing nothing. Cost each over the whole life of the project, not just the build.","Discount future costs to today's value so options are compared fairly: present value = cost / (1 + rate) raised to the power of the number of years (for example, with a 5% rate, AED 100 paid in 2 years is worth 100 / 1.05 / 1.05 = about AED 90.70 today).","Add the value of risks each option leaves with the public body, such as delay or cost overrun.","Value for money means the partnership has a lower total cost, or better service for the same cost, once risks are included."],
  "Public delivery costs AED 100,000,000 in building plus AED 6,000,000 a year for 20 years: 100,000,000 + 20 x 6,000,000 = AED 220,000,000, before risk and discounting. Partnership: AED 12,500,000 a year for 20 years = AED 250,000,000. The partnership is AED 30,000,000 higher, so it only makes sense if the transferred risk is worth more than AED 30,000,000.",
  "Cost two options for a sample school project over 20 years and decide if the partnership passes, showing the risk value used.","Give all costs units and years; state and explain the risk value; support the decision with the numbers."),
 ("Allocate risk and write enforceable terms","Evaluate",9,"Each risk should sit with the party best able to control it. Clear terms decide who pays when something goes wrong.",
  ["Build a risk register: design, construction delay, demand, operation, change in law, and force majeure (events outside anyone's control, such as a major flood). Mark who bears each one.","Give construction and operating risks to the private partner and policy or legal-change risks to the public body.","Include payment deductions for poor service, a step-in right (the public body may take over the service) if the partner fails, and a clear exit and hand-back process.","Follow the procurement rules for public money: fair competition, written evaluation criteria and recorded decisions."],
  "A road is promised for 24 months. A delay of 3 months with a daily penalty of AED 20,000 for 90 days costs the partner 90 x 20,000 = AED 1,800,000. Without the penalty clause the public body would bear that loss in lost use.",
  "Draw a risk register of at least 8 risks for a sample hospital partnership, assign each to a party and justify it in one line.","Give each risk one owner and a reason; describe deduction and step-in terms; list procurement steps."),
 ("Monitor delivery and review the partnership","Analyse",9,"After signing, governance is mostly checking. Regular monitoring catches small failures before they become large ones.",
  ["Set measurable service standards, such as availability, response time and cleanliness scores, with a clear way to measure each.","Track milestones against the plan and record causes of delay.","Apply payment deductions exactly as the contract says, and keep a written record of every decision.","Report to the public body and the public in plain words, and hold a formal review at fixed points, for example every 5 years."],
  "A building must be available 98.0% of 8,760 hours in a year = 8,584.8 hours. Actual availability is 8,410 hours, which is 96.0%. Shortfall = 174.8 hours below the standard, so the deduction clause applies for that period.",
  "Calculate performance against 4 standards from a sample monthly report and draft a short notice of any deduction.","Measure each standard with units; let the deduction follow the contract clause; date the notice and keep it factual."),
],
}
